      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective cohort study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the novel treatment procedure with nonoperative treatment for low back pain (LBP) in elderly patients with degenerative lumbar scoliosis (DLS)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Treatment of LBP associated with elderly DLS is controversial. We developed a novel treatment procedure, termed percutaneous intervertebral-vacuum polymethylmethacrylate injection (PIPI)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We included patients with de novo DLS aged ≥65 years who had LBP with a visual analogue scale (VAS) score of >50 for ≥6 months with intervertebral vacuum and vertebral bone marrow edema (BME) defined on fat-saturated T2-weighted or gadolinium-enhanced T1-weighted magnetic resonance imaging. The primary outcomes were evaluated using the VAS score and modified Oswestry Disability Index (ODI). As an objective measurement, we scored BME on magnetic resonance imaging."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Between August 2004 and July 2011, 109 patients underwent PIPI and 53 received nonoperative treatment. At 1 month, mean improvements in VAS scores were -55.3 (95% CI, -60.5 to -50.1) and -1.9 (CI, -7.7 to 3.8) and mean improvements in ODI were -22.7 (CI, -27.3 to -18.2) and -0.6 (CI, -6.6 to 5.4) for the PIPI and nonoperative groups, respectively. At 2 years, mean improvements in VAS scores were -52.2 (CI, -59.9 to -44.4) and -4.0 (CI, -10.9 to 3.0) and mean improvements in ODI were -20.7 (CI, -27.3 to -14.5) and -1.0 (CI, -7.7 to 5.7) for the PIPI and nonoperative groups, respectively. BME substantially decreased in the PIPI group compared with the nonoperative group (P\u200a<0.001) and correlated with VAS score and ODI improvements (VAS score: r\u200a=\u200a0.502, P\u200a<0.001; ODI: r\u200a=\u200a0.372, P\u200a<0.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "PIPI improved treatment for LBP, with a sustained clinical benefit for at least 2 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "LEVEL OF EVIDENCE",
          "content": 3
        }
      ]},
